<%# The tally of orders still waiting. Rendered twice per page — once in the
    sidebar, once in the mobile top bar, which is the only one of the two a
    phone can see without opening the drawer — hence the `id` local: two live
    copies need two Turbo Stream targets, and a duplicate id would leave one of
    them permanently stale.

    The wrapper is always rendered, empty or not: it is what the poller replaces
    by id, and a target that only exists once there is something to count is a
    target the first order of the evening can never land on.

    data-pending-count is the browser's half of the exchange — the poller reads
    it back off the page and sends it with the next request, so the server can
    answer "nothing has moved" without rendering anything at all.

    The figure is written twice on purpose. A bare number sitting beside a nav
    label says nothing on its own to a screen reader, so the digit is hidden
    from the accessibility tree and the sentence is given instead. %>
<span id="<%= id %>" class="ml-auto" data-pending-count="<%= count %>">
  <% if count.positive? %>
    <span class="badge badge-sm badge-warning num" aria-hidden="true"><%= count %></span>
    <span class="sr-only"><%= t("owner.orders.pending_count", count: count) %></span>
  <% end %>
</span>
